<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes Around Us</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I See a Rectangle.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Use gestures and demonstrations to help classmates perform a simple task. Concept: Use a word and a gesture or drawing to describe a familiar object.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use gestures and simple words to describe shapes to classmates (e.g., using hands to represent the shape of a circle). Can use a combination of drawings and words to clarify their responses about shapes (e.g., drawing the shape of a triangle and writing the word next to it). Can describe shapes using body language and pointing during classroom activities (e.g., miming a square shape by using the arms to create straight lines).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Shape Walk" activity. Students stand up, and the teacher calls out a shape (e.g., "Find a circle!"). Students point to an object in the classroom that is that shape, using the phrase "I see a circle!" while making a circle shape with their hands. This activates prior knowledge of shapes and the target phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to describe shapes using gestures and simple words. For example, the teacher holds up a square flashcard and says, "This is a square," while tracing a square in the air with their fingers. Then, the teacher points to a square object (e.g., a book) and says, "Look! A square book!" demonstrating pointing and a simple sentence. The teacher clearly pronounces /s/ in "square" and /r/ in "rectangle."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a circle flashcard) "Is this a square?" (Students shake heads/say "No!") "What shape is it?" (Students say "Circle!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Miming a rectangle with arms) "Can you show me a rectangle with your hands?" (Students mime the shape).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shapes Around Us")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher introduces the "Shape Collage" project, explaining its steps using visual aids, simple words, and gestures. The focus is on mediating the understanding of the project's instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Example "Shape Collage" (made by teacher), large pictures of objects (e.g., a square cracker, a round clock, a rectangular door), scissors, glue, construction paper. Visuals of simple steps: "1. Cut shapes," "2. Glue shapes," "3. Label shapes."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying shapes: Recognize and name shapes like squares and circles during classroom activities. Describing shapes: Use simple phrases to talk about shapes (e.g., "This is a rectangle.") and use gestures/demonstrations to clarify the instructions for the project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a picture of a collage) "What are we making?" (A collage!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows scissors) "What do we use to cut?" (Scissors!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to shapes) "What shapes do we see?" (Rectangle, Circle, Square!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar: Shape + noun (e.g., "a square table"), Present simple (e.g., "That is a circle.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vocabulary: square, rectangle, circle, triangle, see, look, draw, find, it, this, that.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation: /s/ in "square", /r/ in "rectangle"; initial sounds.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Gestural Shape Description:</b> The teacher holds up a shape flashcard (e.g., a circle). Students must make the shape with their hands/body and say "This is a circle." The teacher then points to an object of that shape in the classroom and asks, "What shape is it?" encouraging students to use the target phrase and gesture. This is repeated for square, rectangle, and triangle.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Show and Explain" Activity:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a small bag with paper cutouts of different shapes (circle, square, rectangle, triangle). Student A picks a shape, shows it to Student B, and says "This is a [shape]." Student A then uses gestures or points to an object in the classroom that has that shape (e.g., "Look! A square door!"). Student B affirms or corrects using gestures. They swap roles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Draw and Describe" Mediation:</b> Students are given whiteboards or paper. The teacher says a shape name (e.g., "rectangle"). Students draw the shape. Then, the teacher asks a student to "explain" their drawing to a classmate using gestures and words (e.g., drawing a rectangle, pointing to its sides, and saying "This is long, this is short. It is a rectangle."). The classmate attempts to draw a similar shape based on the explanation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shapes Around Us")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in small groups (3-4 students) to create their "Shape Collage." The task involves peer mediation to accomplish the project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Collaborative project work with peer explanation/demonstration.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group receives a large sheet of paper, magazines with pictures, scissors, and glue.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students cut out pictures of objects that are circles, squares, or rectangles. As they cut, they practice mediating by showing a picture to a peer and asking, "What shape is it?" or saying, "This is a rectangle," using gestures to clarify.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When gluing, students help each other by pointing to where a shape should go or demonstrating how to hold the glue stick.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, students help each other label the shapes on their collage with simple sentences like "This is a circle." (The teacher circulates, facilitating and prompting mediation, e.g., "Can you show your friend how to write 'square'?" or "How can you tell your friend that picture is a rectangle?").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> The "Shape Collage" directly incorporates "I See a Rectangle" by having students identify and label various shapes found in everyday objects, demonstrating their understanding of "Shapes Around Us" through a tangible product and peer interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their "Shape Collage" to the class or another group. Students take turns pointing to a shape on their collage, stating "This is a [shape]," and using gestures to demonstrate the shape (e.g., miming a square). They also demonstrate how they helped a classmate by explaining a part of the collage-making process (e.g., "I cut the circle," showing the cutting motion). The teacher observes their mediation skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of shape names (e.g., /s/ in "square").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (square, rectangle, circle, "This is a...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of gestures/demonstrations to convey shape or task.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration: Helping classmates perform a simple task (e.g., finding a picture, gluing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to use drawings/words to clarify responses about shapes.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick drawing activity. They draw one shape they put on the collage and write its name. Below it, they complete two simple sentences:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can show a ____________ with my hands."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can tell my friend 'This is a ____________'."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy for you about showing shapes with your hands?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was hard about helping your friend understand about the shapes in the collage?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson, particularly regarding students' ability to use gestures and simple words for mediation?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with explaining or demonstrating shapes to their peers? (e.g., more direct modeling, specific sentence frames).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the "Shape Collage" effectively engage students in using the target language and mediation skills?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This is the final lesson in the "Shapes Around Us" theme. The next lesson will begin a new theme, building upon the foundational communication skills (gestures, simple sentences) developed in this unit and introducing new vocabulary and concepts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>